uations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(4,-6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a1fae89-5862-5273-b352-1b55c259e49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534408} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7dc81c-912e-5bf3-988e-249f3367e151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534415} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecba329-64f6-5352-93a3-b9c05b3611c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534494} {:answer/id #uuid "98e0b29e-8625-5698-b82d-e8fb994ff0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534607} {:answer/id #uuid "a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a28191ad-4a5a-576f-bfda-4f31c85b6f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534633} :answer-field/id #uuid "5908b398-7b43-5b62-978f-3d17f21f0446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1d2632a-4694-5c22-b050-b15669bba222" :question/math-academy-id "q-79167" :question/problem "![](images/15/158ce9317d062a090ed36bda7361d025e4c8f4c68ea43484f50015f01c411a30.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5c/5cc99d78c5fe5afacde4ba96e2a4010b0fab66bdabbe5c2b518bd936f36c7d84.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d7c966-92e3-54fd-81d7-481aa35aaa09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345529} {:answer/id #uuid "2686f739-5930-5502-bba3-f9bfa090fa61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345697} {:answer/id #uuid "3e956404-dd4e-52f8-a622-3b3af34ad2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345884} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5f0f9-d70f-5912-8a4d-ca0220cf783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347120} {:answer/id #uuid "f599c6f4-8d97-5337-8293-400437297d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d7c966-92e3-54fd-81d7-481aa35aaa09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345529} :answer-field/id #uuid "783ab479-2f74-5756-bcf6-43567d8f1a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91506c48-d81a-5a96-ada5-d036ea592b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-79168" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0ec11769fa20feb317ef502f41e1d31ffe539d4b17a51ad46b8487c17752021.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/97/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f11f8f5-14af-5418-a30f-2cdb361b260a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346029} {:answer/id #uuid "60d87398-f4f2-5513-9687-a427c1be6407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346190} {:answer/id #uuid "6661a258-7ca8-5569-93ca-fc1dce78c2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346245} {:answer/id #uuid "80d564fe-11e4-5e17-ae8d-364ca396dbfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346476} {:answer/id #uuid "d68fa614-0fdb-5265-9177-7d915486a0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d87398-f4f2-5513-9687-a427c1be6407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346190} :answer-field/id #uuid "00426326-692f-5b6d-a52b-4521d86868ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c8948ed-478d-597d-8d84-3303f297f4dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79192" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb599ef-8a81-52a5-8b28-084af574bb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345442} {:answer/id #uuid "39105a8b-6d43-5d27-870f-eea96d04d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345837} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e4691-b8a5-59dd-812a-632868d6034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346929} {:answer/id #uuid "b869bfc2-922d-50cc-8561-1dc9c77a11db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346955} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fac58b-c9cb-5f3e-8265-5ba360717b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b869bfc2-922d-50cc-8561-1dc9c77a11db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346955} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2a1c5c-432e-561b-bbf6-4b53c4971d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9c6c4b8-31e2-55b5-bdea-fe6d4fa5f314" :question/math-academy-id "q-79219" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =49({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =49(1) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49.$"}]}
